WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.860
Acetylcholine slows the heart.

2
00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:10.700
The same acetylcholine makes the muscle around your airways contract.

3
00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.300
Who decides the answer, the molecule or the cell?

4
00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:19.460
The molecule carries no instruction about the answer.

5
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:25.600
Each cell decides, through its receptor and the G protein that receptor hands the

6
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:26.600
signal to.

7
00:00:26.883 --> 00:00:32.623
This receptor is a G-protein-coupled receptor, or GPCR.

8
00:00:33.283 --> 00:00:37.883
Its chain crosses the membrane seven times, as seven helices.

9
00:00:38.963 --> 00:00:43.363
Acetylcholine is an agonist, a ligand that activates its receptor.

10
00:00:44.143 --> 00:00:49.903
It binds in a pocket among the helices and shifts them a little outside and a lot

11
00:00:49.903 --> 00:00:55.763
inside. The inner end of helix 6 swings outward and opens a cavity.

12
00:00:57.467 --> 00:01:04.787
Waiting just inside the membrane is a G protein, and its Gα part holds GDP.

13
00:01:05.867 --> 00:01:12.267
Gβ and Gγ bind each other so tightly that they act as one unit,

14
00:01:12.267 --> 00:01:19.107
βγ. The active receptor grips the G protein and loosens Gα's hold on GDP.

15
00:01:20.347 --> 00:01:24.087
GDP leaves, and GTP takes the empty site.

16
00:01:24.087 --> 00:01:30.827
In the textbook picture, Gα with GTP then lets go of the receptor and of

17
00:01:30.827 --> 00:01:31.827
βγ.

18
00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:40.130
The cell has two messengers, Gα with GTP and free βγ, and each can find a

19
00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:44.950
target. In pacemaker cells of the heart, the receptor is M2.

20
00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:48.530
It couples to a G protein called Gi.

21
00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:53.510
The freed βγ binds a potassium channel and opens it.

22
00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:58.830
Potassium flows out, the membrane potential moves away from the firing threshold,

23
00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:00.090
and the heart slows.

24
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.440
In airway muscle, the receptor is M3.

25
00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:08.440
It couples to a different G protein, called Gq.

26
00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:14.580
Gα with GTP switches on an enzyme, phospholipase C-β.

27
00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:18.180
The enzyme cuts PIP2.

28
00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:22.800
IP3 releases stored calcium, and the muscle contracts.

29
00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:29.880
In the heart, acetylcholine meets M2 and Gi, and the heart slows.

30
00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:36.120
In the airways, it meets M3 and Gq, and the muscle contracts.

31
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.160
The acetylcholine is the same.

32
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:43.440
The receptor and the G protein it couples to are different.

33
00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:50.400
So keep this rule: the receptor and its G protein decide the answer, not the signal.
